<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perugino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1523">1523</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Pietro Perugino, Pietro Vannucci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Perugino, born Pietro Vannucci, was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painter of the Umbrian school who worked mainly in Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Known for calm, balanced compositions and gentle figures, he painted the Delivery of the Keys in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and decorated the Collegio del Cambio in Perugia. The young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> trained in his workshop.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perugino.jpg" alt="Perugino" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Perugino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Perugino_031.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Città della Pieve, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Fontignano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.95,12&amp;year=1497" aria-label="Città della Pieve on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4840 638 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Perugino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5290 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5290 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.95,12&amp;year=1497">What was here in 1497? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
